<h1>Matrix Dimensions Randomization</h1>
<p>A dimension does not have to list its values. It can describe how to draw them instead: a few numbers from a
	range, a few entries from a pool, or a few strings built from a character set. This page follows
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-cases-exclusion/">Matrix Cases Exclusion</a> and uses the
	<strong>Matrix Dimensions Randomization</strong> test of the <strong>Advanced Configuration Parameters</strong>
	group.</p>
<p>What stays fixed is the <em>number</em> of values each dimension contributes. The matrix therefore produces the
	same number of cases on every configuration, which is what lets the controller plan a randomized test the way it
	plans any other, while the values themselves change from one draw to the next.</p>
<p>Select the <strong>Matrix Dimensions Randomization</strong> test and set its <strong>Maze Executable
	Folder</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/matrix-dimensions-randomization-config.png", "Matrix Dimensions Randomization run panel reporting Total Cases 48, with the seed, height, width_ratio and illegal_arg column headers marked with a dice icon and filled with drawn values", "large") }}
<p>The panel reports <strong>Total Cases: 48</strong>. Four of the six column headers, <strong>seed</strong>,
	<strong>height</strong>, <strong>width_ratio</strong> and <strong>illegal_arg</strong>, carry a dice icon: these
	dimensions drew their values when the panel was built. Here <strong>seed</strong> drew <code>100</code> and
	<code>2024</code> from its pool, <strong>height</strong> drew <code>15</code> and <code>9</code>,
	<strong>width_ratio</strong> drew two floating-point numbers and <strong>illegal_arg</strong> drew
	<code>GM</code> and <code>G</code>. Another panel would show other values, but always two per dimension.</p>

<h2 id="defining-random-dimensions">Defining random dimensions</h2>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_matrix_dimensions_randomization/") }}
<p>The random dimensions are declared with the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/matrix/#dimensions"><code>Dimension</code></a> class methods, next to two
	ordinary dimensions that list their values:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('matrix = OrcraftConfigField.Matrix(
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension(label="command", values=("generate", "solve")),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension(label="algorithm", values=(None, "BFS")),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension.random_choice(label="seed", values=(1, 7, 42, 100, 2024), size=2),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension.random(label="height", min_value=5, max_value=15, size=2),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension.random(label="width_ratio", min_value=1.5, max_value=2.0, size=2),
	OrcraftConfigField.Dimension.random_string(label="illegal_arg", min_length=1, max_length=2, size=2, charset=" MG"),

	random_order=True,

	exclude=[
		lambda case: case.params["command"] == "generate" and case.params["algorithm"] is not None,
	],
)') }}
<p>Each constructor describes a draw and the <code>size</code> of it:</p>
<ul>
	<li><code>random_choice</code> takes <code>size</code> values from the <code>values</code> pool, without
		repeating one.</li>
	<li><code>random</code> takes <code>size</code> numbers from the inclusive range between <code>min_value</code>
		and <code>max_value</code>. Integer bounds give integers, as for <strong>height</strong>; floating-point bounds
		give floating-point numbers, as for <strong>width_ratio</strong>.</li>
	<li><code>random_string</code> takes <code>size</code> strings whose length lies between <code>min_length</code>
		and <code>max_length</code>, built from <code>charset</code>. A single <code>length</code> can be given
		instead of the two bounds, and without a <code>charset</code> letters and digits are used.</li>
</ul>
<p>The declaration fixes the case count. Two commands, two algorithms and four dimensions of two values give
	<code>2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 64</code> combinations, and the <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-cases-exclusion/">exclusion rule</a>
	removes the sixteen <code>generate</code> cases that name an algorithm, leaving 48. The rule refers only to the
	two listed dimensions, so it removes the same sixteen cases whatever the random dimensions draw and the count
	stays 48. A rule that looked at a random value could remove a different number of cases on each draw, and the
	panel warns about that combination. The <code>random_order</code> option is covered
	<a class="text-accent" href="#run-order">below</a>.</p>

<h3 id="using-the-drawn-values">Using the drawn values</h3>
<p>The drawn values reach <code>_run_case</code> through <code>case.params</code> like any other dimension value.
	The test derives the maze width from the height and the ratio, and appends the random string to the command:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

	maze_app = MazeApp(
		self.config.maze_executable_folder,
		(case.params["command"] + case.params["illegal_arg"]).strip(),
		width=round(case.params["height"] * case.params["width_ratio"]),
		height=case.params["height"],
		seed=case.params["seed"],
		algorithm=case.params["algorithm"],
	)

	maze_app.run()

	return case.result') }}
<p>A command such as <code>generateGM</code> is not one the Maze application knows, and its complaint appears in the
	case log. The case still passes, because this test only exercises the values and checks nothing. Turning an
	application's answer into a case status is the subject of the <strong>Statuses and Results</strong> group later in
	the tutorial.</p>

<h2 id="the-random-configuration">The random configuration</h2>
<p>Click the <strong>seed</strong> column header. The <strong>Dimension Filter</strong> dialog introduced in
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/filtering-matrix-dimensions/">Filtering Matrix Dimensions</a> opens, but
	for a random dimension its lower half, <strong>Random Configuration</strong>, is where the dimension is
	defined:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/random-choice-dialog.png", "Dimension Filter dialog for the seed dimension listing the drawn values 100 and 2024, with Random Configuration set to Random Choice, the values pool 1, 7, 42, 100, 2024 and a size of 2", "large") }}
<p><strong>Mode</strong> reads <strong>Random Choice</strong>, <strong>Values</strong> holds the pool declared in the
	source and <strong>Size</strong> the number of values to draw. The list above shows the two values this draw
	produced, both checked. <strong>Select All</strong> and <strong>Clear All</strong> are disabled and the
	<strong>Add value</strong> field is absent: the list is the result of the draw, not a set of choices to narrow.</p>
<p>The <strong>height</strong> dimension shows the numeric form of the same dialog:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/random-number-dialog.png", "Dimension Filter dialog for the height dimension listing the drawn values 15 and 9, with Random Configuration set to Random Number, Min 5, Max 15 and a size of 2", "large") }}
<p><strong>Random Number</strong> exposes the <strong>Min</strong> and <strong>Max</strong> bounds and the
	<strong>Size</strong>. The <strong>illegal_arg</strong> dimension shows the string form:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/random-string-dialog.png", "Dimension Filter dialog for the illegal_arg dimension listing the drawn values G and GM, with Random Configuration set to Random String, a length between 1 and 2, a size of 2 and the charset MG", "large") }}
<p><strong>Random String</strong> exposes <strong>Min Length</strong>, <strong>Max Length</strong>,
	<strong>Size</strong> and <strong>Charset</strong>. The charset declared in the source is <code>" MG"</code> with
	a leading space; the strings are stripped of it in <code>_run_case</code>, which is how a draw can also produce a
	plain <code>generate</code> with no suffix at all.</p>

<h3 id="drawing-again">Drawing again</h3>
<p>The specification is the configuration. The values it happened to draw are not, so a run does not draw new
	values by itself: the values on the panel are the values the run uses, and a second run of the same panel uses
	them again. To draw again, open the dimension's dialog and click <strong class="text-accent">Apply</strong> or
	<strong>OK</strong>, even with nothing changed. The dimension is drawn afresh, the case table is rebuilt around
	the new values and the plan is marked as changed until <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong> commits
	it.</p>

<h3 id="changing-the-specification">Changing the specification</h3>
<p>Every field of the <strong>Random Configuration</strong> group can be edited for this configuration. Set the
	<strong>seed</strong> dimension's <strong>Size</strong> to <code>3</code> and click <strong>OK</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/seed-size-three.png", "Matrix Dimensions Randomization run panel after drawing three seeds, reporting Total Cases 72 with the seed column header and the Total Cases counter marked as changed", "large") }}
<p>Three seeds instead of two make <strong>Total Cases: 72</strong>, and both the <strong>seed</strong> header and
	the counter carry the <code>🔸</code> badge because the panel no longer matches the committed plan. The
	<strong>Mode</strong> itself can be changed too, so a dimension declared with a pool can be re-specified as a
	numeric range from the controller, and <strong>Disabled</strong> turns the dimension back into a plain list of
	the values currently drawn. <strong>Reset to Defaults</strong> inside the dialog restores the declared
	specification, and the panel's own <strong>Reset To Defaults</strong> restores every dimension at once.</p>

<h3 id="run-order">Run order</h3>
<p>The <strong>Run Order</strong> control above the case table decides the order in which the selected cases run.
	<strong>In Order</strong> runs them by case number. <strong>Random (New)</strong> shuffles them with a fresh seed,
	and the <strong>Order</strong> column shows the position each case will take. Once a shuffled run exists,
	<strong>Random (Last)</strong> repeats its order. The matrix declares <code>random_order=True</code> to ask for a
	shuffled order by default; the value is part of the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/matrix/#matrix-options">matrix options</a>, together with the seed options
	that make a shuffled run reproducible.</p>

<h2 id="running-the-drawn-cases">Running the drawn cases</h2>
<p>With the declared specification in place, click <strong class="text-accent">Update Plan</strong>, then
	<strong class="text-purple">Run</strong> and confirm with <strong>Yes</strong>. The test reports 48 planned, done
	and passed cases, and the log's result table records the drawn value of every dimension for every case:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/running-matrix-dimensions-randomization-test.png", "Completed Matrix Dimensions Randomization run with all 48 cases passing and the log listing the drawn seed, height, width_ratio and illegal_arg of each case", "large") }}
<p>The log is what makes a random run reproducible after the fact. A case that fails in a soak run can be re-run by
	hand with the exact values the table shows, and the full floating-point value of <strong>width_ratio</strong>
	appears here even where the panel had to truncate it.</p>

<h3 id="inspecting-the-run">Inspecting the run</h3>
<p>Click <strong>Details</strong> for the completed run. The configuration panel on the left has a
	<strong>Random Dimensions</strong> table that records the specification of each random dimension, with its
	default, planned and run values side by side: the mode, the pool or range, the length bounds, the size and the
	charset. The listed dimensions keep their own table below it:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/matrix-dimensions-randomization/matrix-dimensions-randomization-details.png", "Matrix Dimensions Randomization Details view with a Random Dimensions table recording the mode, pool, range, size and charset of each random dimension for the default, plan and run, and a results tree with 48 passing cases", "large") }}
<p>The dialog records the specification because that is what was configured. The values the run drew are in the case
	table further down and in the log, so both halves of a random run, the rule and the outcome, can be inspected
	later.</p>

<p>The values on this page are drawn by the framework. A dimension can also obtain its values from a function the
	test supplies, which is what
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-lambda-dimensions/">Matrix Lambda Dimensions</a> covers next.</p>
